## Global Design Decisions

**The 3D viewer is always the hero.** Never shrink it below 60% of the screen. All explanation happens either in 3D space (labels, arrows, glowing planes) or in large-type prose at the bottom of the viewer — not in side panels.

**No cold blue dashboard aesthetic.** Use a warmer dark palette: deep slate backgrounds, amber/gold for the crystal atoms (they're material, not space), cyan for the dislocation core, red for blocked boundaries, green for transmitted ones. The current palette reads "developer tool." This should read "science museum installation."

**In-scene annotations, not UI overlays.** Labels for "dislocation," "grain boundary," "slip plane" should be anchored in 3D space and move with the crystal as you orbit it. Not floating CSS text in front of the canvas.

**The narration strip.** One sentence at a time, at the bottom of the 3D viewer, large type (20–24px), appearing with a subtle fade. No scrollable text blocks. No reading walls.

**The experiment log.** A small collapsible drawer in the bottom-left: every simulation the student ran is logged with grain size, material, result. In Chapter 4 these become the data points. It makes the student feel like a real experimenter.

**Responsive rotation.** The 3D crystal should auto-rotate gently when idle (like a product showcase). Click to orbit manually. The rotation stops the moment the simulation starts.

**Sound design (optional but powerful).** A very subtle crystalline click when the dislocation hits a boundary. Nothing game-like — just enough to reinforce the visual event.

