## Navigation Model

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ●──────────○──────────○──────────○    [Research →] │
│  Defect    Wall    Strength    Equation              │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

- Filled circle = current chapter
- Hollow = unlocked but not visited
- Grayed = locked (must complete previous)
- **[Research →]** always visible in top right — escape hatch to the advanced dashboard at any point for the student who wants to run ahead

---

## What This Redesign Solves

| Current problem | Redesign fix |
|---|---|
| Equation before intuition | Chapter 4 is the last thing they see |
| Jargon on landing | No technical terms until Chapter 2, and only after the concept is visual |
| Static chart with no meaning | Student builds the chart themselves |
| No in-scene labels | All annotations anchor in 3D space |
| Overwhelming dashboard as entry | Dashboard is the optional exit, not the entry |
| Student is passive viewer | Student runs experiments, makes predictions, owns the data |

---

## What to Build First

If I were you, I'd prototype Chapter 1 alone as a proof of concept — full screen 3D viewer, in-scene annotations, the narration strip, the single-button flow. Get that feeling right. The rest of the chapters use the same visual language. Once Chapter 1 feels like a museum installation and not a research tool, you'll know the design direction is correct.

The engine you've already built can power all four chapters without modification. The UI layer is the only thing changing.